# File: specs/347-deliver-generated-worker-prompts-exactly-once-before-settlement/feature.gherkin
#
# Generated from: specs/347-deliver-generated-worker-prompts-exactly-once-before-settlement/requirements.md
Feature: Deliver generated worker prompts exactly once before settlement
  Fresh execute sibling workers could settle after only com.nmg-sdlc.run
  and be recorded as missing_handoff before the generated prompt arrived.
  This was fixed by delivering that prompt exactly once to a live session
  before observation.

  # --- Bug Is Fixed ---

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: Bug is fixed
    Given execute submits one generated prompt to a fresh standard, review, or remediation --kind omp worker
    When prompt submission returns before working, blocked, or a valid expected handoff proves activation
    Then execute persists activating before bounded activation and persists delivered only after activation proof
    And initial idle or done cannot settle or close the pane

  # --- Related Behavior Still Works ---

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario: No regression
    Given a matching retained worker is pending, activating, or proven delivered
    When execute resumes or finishes observing that worker
    Then pending may dispatch once, activating resumes bounded activation without another prompt, and delivered receives no second prompt
    And activation exhaustion becomes retained prompt_pending
    And a still-missing handoff after proven activation remains missing_handoff and still closes the owned pane unless --retain-worker was requested
    And review prompt content and review evidence validation remain unchanged

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: Controller crash after accepted prompt resumes activation exactly once
    Given a fresh worker accepted its generated prompt and the checkpoint persisted activating
    When the controller exits before bounded activation completes and the next invocation resumes
    Then the next invocation re-enters bounded activation without invoking agentPrompt
    And working, blocked, or a valid expected handoff transitions the worker to delivered
